<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsewang Dorji Namjal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsewang Dorji Namjal was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> from 1745 to 1750, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a> while still a youth. His erratic rule alienated the nobility, and in 1750 he was overthrown and blinded by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a>. His fall began the succession struggles that left the khanate open to the Qing conquest of 1755–1757.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tsewang Dorji Namjal was born in 1732, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a>, ruler of the Dzungar Khanate, which covered most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and parts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. His father left a strong state. A form of peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> had held since 1739, and the trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> that followed widened the khanate's economic base. Galdan Tseren had also brought Turkic Taranchi people into the fertile oases to build irrigation systems, and although tension with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> grew in his last years, an influential faction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> valued an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> as a counterweight to China.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a> had three sons and a daughter. At his death the eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a>, was 17, Tsewang Dorji Namjal 13 and the youngest, Tsewang Dashi, seven. The father's will named Tsewang Dorji Namjal as his successor, and in 1746 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> nobility proclaimed him their leader.</p><p>Little is known of him. The surviving sources describe him in his youth as perverse, cruel and paranoid, a heavy drinker who was more interested in killing dogs than in affairs of state; one says that he liked to roam the villages drinking chang and seducing girls. Several missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> further weakened his standing among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> elite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a> conspired with the husband of their sister Ulam Bayar, the Baghatur Sayin Bolek, and with other saisans, the high officials of the khanate, to kill Tsewang Dorji Namjal on a hunting trip. During a failed attack on the plotters he was captured and blinded, and he was sent as a prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> together with his brother Tsewang Dashi. He died in 1750. The internal struggle for power that had begun after his father's death led to anarchy and, in the end, to the destruction of the khanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsewang Dorji Namjal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Tsewang Dorji Namjal</b> was overthrown and blinded in 1750 by his half-brother Lama Dorji, who was himself killed in 1753 by Dawachi, a grandson of Tsering Dondub, with the help of the Khoit prince Amursana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
